From b5e0f8b2c09b1d0c4428aed999e450fd13b70dc3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dsv4-a5 <soc.yuan@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 13:14:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 02/10] [perf](kt-kernel): stripe worker binding over CCDs,
 compact MXFP4 E8M0 scales, runtime phase timing

Three independent CPU-side changes for the single-card A5 DeepSeek-V4-Flash
offload, all measured standalone (no NPU touched) on 2x EPYC 9575F (Zen5,
2 NUMA, 8 CCDs per socket, AVX512-BF16, no AMX).

1. worker_pool.cpp: bind workers striped over L3 domains instead of packed.

   hwloc's "logical core i inside the NUMA cpuset" is dense, so the first 8
   workers of a node all land on CCD0. A single CCD sustains ~76 GB/s on this
   host while the socket does ~246 and both sockets do ~495. MXFP4 decode
   dispatches only nth*experts*2 tasks (16 for the modal k=2 at E=96) and
   `worker_count = min(restricted, task_num)` wakes exactly that many workers,
   so the decode path systematically confined itself to CCD0/CCD1.

   Worker i now goes to L3 domain (i % ndomains), slot (i / ndomains);
   over-subscription falls onto SMT siblings instead of leaving the thread
   unbound. KT_CPU_BIND=pack restores the historical order for A/B without a
   rebuild. The cache holds duplicated bitmaps rather than hwloc objects
   because each call site loads its own topology.

   Measured, E=96 / 96 threads / tp=2 / qlen=1, back-to-back A/B:
     per-layer expected cost  116.2 -> 94.6 us   (-18.6%, = -0.93 ms/token
                                                  over 43 MoE layers)
     T(k=1)  92.9/94.9 -> 59.1/59.9 us  (-36%)
     T(k=2) 117.6/119.5 -> 91.6/97.3 us (-20%)
     T(k=6) unchanged: 48 tasks already cover all 8 CCDs either way.
   The whole win lands in the gate/up phase (78.0 -> 49.5 us at k=2), which is
   exactly the phase with the fewest tasks. Larger at low thread counts:
   -33% at 16 threads, -25% at 32.

2. fp4-moe.hpp / common.hpp: store E8M0 scales as one byte per k-group.

   BufferB reserved one FP32 per 32 weights, but finalize_scale_e8() has
   always compacted those to one exponent byte in place right after loading
   and the kernel only ever reads the byte (get_scale_e8). The remaining 3
   bytes were resident and never touched: 2.25 of 15.014 MiB per expert per
   layer, 15.0%.

   Allocation happens before the data is known, so the FP32 fallback cannot be
   chosen after the fact; this is therefore opt-in via the new
   GeneralMOEConfig::compact_mxfp4_scales (default false, same style as
   skip_gpu_expert_weights). In compact mode `d` is set to null so any FP32
   scale read is a null dereference rather than an out-of-bounds one, and the
   new load_scales_bf16() takes the exponent straight from the BF16 bit
   pattern, throwing (with the KT_MXFP4_COMPACT_SCALES=0 escape hatch named in
   the message) if a scale is not a positive power of two. The non-compact
   path is byte-for-byte the previous sequence. python/utils/amx.py turns it
   on for method="MXFP4".

   Measured resident memory, one layer, 256 logical experts:
     marginal MiB/expert/layer  15.013 -> 12.758   (-15.0%, matches
                                n*k/2 + n*k/32 = 12.75 exactly)
     43-layer projection  E=96: 61.33 -> 52.25 GiB,  E=128: 81.77 -> 69.66
   At a fixed host-RAM budget this extends the reachable offload:
     80 GiB: 126 -> 149 experts;  100 GiB: 158 -> 186;  115 GiB: 182 -> 214.

   Numerics on DeepSeek-V4-Flash-0731 layer 3, non-prefix mask {0,2,4,6}
   resident, against an independent float32 dequantisation: rel Frobenius
   3.922e-03 / 3.860e-03 / 3.798e-03 at tokens=1/8/64 with the compaction on,
   and identical to every printed digit with it off -- the compact byte is the
   same byte finalize_scale_e8 used to extract, and the kernel takes the same
   fp4_mat_vec_kgroup_natural_impl<true> path either way. Negative control
   (nibble swap) still fails at 1.408.

3. moe_base.hpp: make the forward_decode phase timing a runtime switch.

   FORWARD_TIME_PROFILE was a compile-time macro that ships off, so the phase
   breakdown was unavailable in a built wheel. KT_MOE_PHASE_TIMING=1 now
   enables it; resolution moved from microseconds to nanoseconds because a
   bs=1 phase is single-digit microseconds. The guard is one function-local
   static and only protects work already outside the per-task inner loops.

     [kt-phase] layer 3 numa 0 activated 2 | q_input 2.5 | up_gate 49.5 |
                act 0.9 | q_down 1.6 | down 25.8 | weight 2.1 | total 82.8 us

   91% of a decode step is the two GEMM phases; the routing prep, activation,
   weighted sum and cross-NUMA merge together are under 10%.

Build gate 8/8 PASS after a clean rebuild (no AMX tile instructions, AVX512-BF16
and MXFP4 kernel present, no -march=native).

Analysis and the thread/NUMA/offload sweeps are in docs_cpu_optimization.md of
the deployment tree.

